-- CC_0023_monterey_park_council_district_boundaries.sql
-- Give Monterey Park's five council districts REAL geometry, so an address returns the one
-- council member who represents it instead of all five.
--
-- CC_0020 created the five district seats and put the right people in them, but the district
-- rows carried the PLACE geo_id 0648914, because we did not have the boundaries. That was the
-- house pattern (Long Beach holds nine 'District N' rows on one place geo_id) and it kept the
-- seats reachable -- but it answered every Monterey Park address with all five members. This
-- replaces that with the real polygons.
--
-- SOURCE. The city's own ArcGIS Hub, reached through the operationalLayers of its published
-- 'City Council Districts' web map (item d694e535a8d64fe395e2a7c5eff2afa2), exactly the route
-- the Salt Lake County entry in data/arcgis_sources.json documents:
--   https://services9.arcgis.com/ivAMP6vRMPA12GAA/arcgis/rest/services/City_Council_Redistricting/FeatureServer/0
-- Read 2026-08-31. Five polygons, populations 12,084-12,432 -- a balanced adopted map, not a
-- draft scenario.
--
-- 🔴 THE LAYER INDEPENDENTLY CONFIRMS CC_0020'S SEATING. Its CityCouncilMember field reads
-- District 1 Thomas Wong, 2 Elizabeth Yang, 3 Jose Sanchez, 4 Henry Lo, 5 Vinh T. Ngo --
-- which is what CC_0020 seeded from the council WEB PAGE, a different source entirely. Two
-- independent sources agreeing is why the seat-to-person mapping below can be gated on.
--
-- 🔴 THE COVERAGE GATE USES A TOLERANCE, NOT ST_Equals. The district polygons and the TIGER
-- place polygon are two different digitisations of the same city; they will never be equal.
-- The gate asserts the union covers the city to within 2% of its area, and that no two
-- districts overlap by more than a sliver.
--
-- Idempotent: every insert is NOT EXISTS-guarded and the district repoint is guarded on the
-- old place geo_id, so a re-run is a no-op.

BEGIN;

-- District 1, which the city's own layer attributes to Thomas Wong
